        "id": 1002666
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-13T17:42:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I added [a comment](https://issues.gerritcodereview.com/issues/465491615#comment2) to this bug. It seems this is specifically an issue with multi-site 1) not reusing the persisted cache (de-)serialization logic and 2) expecting all caches (including in-memory-only ones) to be serializable even when core doesn\u0027t promise that. This change doesn\u0027t appear to solve either of those issues.",
      "revId": "c9e72f2fccb2228dee077361daa5f15bca463a21",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c90728fb_16b69f8b",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 24,
      "author": {
        "id": 1155599
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-13T18:18:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "multi-site uses Gerrit\u0027s own encoding/decoding mechanism, however, the issue is that the CacheEvictionEvent is defined as\n\n```\npublic CacheEvictionEvent(String cacheName, Object key, String instanceId)\n```\n\nTherefore, gson would serialise an object of type Account.Id as `{\"id\" : 1}`, the information about what type this was initially is therefore lost. When we go to deserialise it, gson will deserialize this to an `Object`, which will create a `Map` of some sort.\n\nNow when we compare this Map to the cache keys, which are of type `Account.Id`, the comparison will fail and caches will never be evicted.\n\nWe could use the className directly, however, for security concerns, we don\u0027t simply want to load any className we receive over the events-broker, therefore we created this class which really is nothing more than a white list of what types can a cache key be.\n\nI\u0027ve followed `EventTypes` in the style and name of the methods, hence the `register` rather than `whitelist` or something.",
      "parentUuid": "50940abb_5e762206",
      "revId": "c9e72f2fccb2228dee077361daa5f15bca463a21",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c650ec94_db08d17d",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 24,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002666
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-13T20:05:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Dani and I had some side chat on this too and I\u0027ll post what I think are the 2 points most of my feedback summarizes to:\n1. The methods that create caches in `CacheModule` already take the types, so if you\u0027re trying to capture that by making callers invoke another method, that seems very wrong. Re-use the info already provided to those methods.\n2. Even if you have the type info, if a plugin creates a cache using a key that\u0027s based on any non-core type that\u0027s complex enough to require a `TypeAdapter`, multi-site still won\u0027t be able to create (deserialize) the object correctly. If you want plugins to be able to tell multi-site about how to do that, then you need to solve this differently (maybe by having multi-site only support plugin caches whose keys implement `Serializable`?).",
      "parentUuid": "c90728fb_16b69f8b",
      "revId": "c9e72f2fccb2228dee077361daa5f15bca463a21",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "67974492_18675703",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 24,
      "author": {
        "id": 1155599
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-14T13:37:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Regarding point 2 - This change is not really about teaching multi-site how to serialize/deserialize anything. We could do that without this class at all, simply using `Class.forName()` would suffice. However, there are potential security concerns with simply reading a class name and loading it, therefore we\u0027d like this to be a sort of \"central register\" of allowed/expected cache key types.\n\nThis is not done to teach Gson about how to serialize/deserialize objects, but just to ensure that we expect to be loading specific classes...\n\n\nI\u0027ll look into what you say in point 1, if there was a way of populating the map without explicitly having to register anything that\u0027d be great.",
      "parentUuid": "c650ec94_db08d17d",
      "revId": "c9e72f2fccb2228dee077361daa5f15bca463a21",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "741fe0d1_b5442d61",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
